B.Yh=new A.H("You can't subclass a bundled localization: their constructors are private. To reuse one, hold an instance and delegate to it, as the example below does.")
B.Yi=new A.H('Wrap text in square brackets to print it as written. The formatter finds tokens anywhere, even inside words, so any Latin word in a pattern usually needs brackets: "at" holds `a` (am/pm), and "day" holds `d`, `a` and `y`.')
B.Yj=new A.H("Year, month, day and hour ranges take a named `isUtc:` and have a `fromDateTime` factory that keeps the timezone of the date you pass. Week ranges take their timezone from the date. Fields out of range roll over the way `DateTime` does.")
B.Yk=new A.H("On a `DateTime`, `endOf(DurationUnit.week)` throws a `MomentException`, because a plain date has no localization to read the week start from. On a `Moment`, it is `endOfLocalWeek()` with the moment's localization: Saturday night for American English, Sunday night for German.")
B.Yl=new A.H("The comparison operators compare instants, so a UTC date and its local copy are equal for `<=` and `>=`. On a `Moment`, `+` and `-` return a `Moment`.")
B.Ym=new A.H("A `DateTime` is either local or UTC. Methods that return a new date return it in the same timezone as the one you called them on: the start of a UTC day is UTC midnight, not local midnight.")
B.Yn=new A.H("A moment created with `localization:` keeps it, whatever the global value is. `hasExplicitLocalization` tells the two kinds apart, and `setLocalization` returns the explicit one, or `null` for a moment that follows the global value.")
B.Yo=new A.H('Both methods also take `form:` for abbreviated output such as "3h ago". See [short forms](/duration/short-forms).')
B.Yp=new A.H("ISO weeks start on Monday, and week 1 is the week with the year's first Thursday. The first days of January can belong to the last week of the previous year, and the last days of December to week 1 of the next. Read `weekYear` together with `week` when you label weeks.")
B.Yr=new A.H("One import gives you the `Moment` class, the `DateTime` extensions, time ranges and all 16 bundled localizations.")
B.Ys=new A.H("Call `toMoment()` on any `DateTime`. Most helpers also work on plain `DateTime` without converting it, so use whichever you have.")
B.Yt=new A.H("`SimpleUnits` has a new `durationUnits` map that `duration()` uses instead of `units`. The built-in localizations use it to write the number in precise durations, while `relative()` keeps the fuzzy wording.")
B.Yu=new A.H("Hour, minute, second and millisecond starts are computed from the instant, not from the wall clock. During the repeated hour when clocks fall back, `startOfHour()` stays in the same copy of the hour as the date you passed. Day, week, month and year starts are built from the calendar fields, so they land on midnight of that day.")
B.Yw=new A.H("Without a clamped year setter, `copyWith(year: \u2026)` on February 29 rolls over to March 1. Move to the first of the month, change the year, then clamp the day back.")
B.Yx=new A.H("Pass a pattern to `format`. Each token in it is replaced with part of the date, and everything else is copied through. Tokens are case-sensitive: `MM` is the month, `mm` the minute. Without a pattern you get `l`, the localization's short date.")
B.Yy=new A.H("Since 6.0 these are `final`. Assigning to them no longer compiles; keep your own variable if you need a different value.")
B.Yz=new A.H("The finders move the calendar date and keep the wall-clock time. Across a daylight saving change, next Monday at 15:09 is still 15:09, even though the real time in between is a week plus or minus an hour. Before 6.0, the result drifted by an hour.")
B.YA=new A.H("English (United States) is the default. Set another one once, when your app starts, and every moment without its own localization uses it. See [global localization](/global-localization).")
B.YB=new A.H("Units smaller than the last unit in the format are not shown. Their time is rounded into the last unit. Since the default format has two units, a duration with three nonzero parts loses the smallest one.")
B.YC=new A.H('The wording steps up through the table below. Each step rounds the duration to its own unit and checks that number, which is also the number that gets printed. That\'s why the switches fall on halves: 44 minutes 30 seconds rounds to 45 minutes, which is past the "minutes" step, so it reads "an hour". Here a month is 30 days and a year is 365 days.')
B.YD=new A.H("The difference shows around daylight saving time. Where clocks fall back at midnight (America/Santiago, for example), 23:00 to 23:59 happens twice. Building 23:59:59.999999 from fields picks the first copy, which can be earlier than the date you started from. The 6.0 result is the real last microsecond of the day.")
B.YE=new A.H("The setters read the month's length from the `lastDayOfMonth` getter, which you can use on its own.")
B.YF=new A.H("These tokens come from the localization, so the order of parts, the separators and the 12- or 24-hour clock change with the language. Use them when you show dates to people instead of writing your own pattern. Lowercase versions are the shorter forms. Most have a getter and a method on `Moment`; the method takes an optional `forceLocal` flag (see below).")
B.YG=new A.H("`Moment` extends `DateTime`, so anything that takes a `DateTime` takes a `Moment`. It's immutable: methods like `add` return a new moment.")
B.YH=new A.H("In 5.x, the methods compared raw fields such as `year`, `month` and `day` without converting. A local date and its UTC copy are the same instant, but their fields differ in most timezones, so they usually didn't match. Now they do:")
B.YI=new A.H("Each unit has a named method, and `startOf(DurationUnit unit)` takes the unit as a value when you pick it at runtime. Use them to round a date down before you store it, group by day or month, or build the lower bound of a query.")
B.YJ=new A.H('The thresholds don\'t change with the form. Words like "an hour" become a number in the short forms, as in the last line.')
B.YK=new A.H("The Arabic (Palestine) localization starts weeks on Saturday, `weekday` 6.")
B.YL=new A.H("Each range type is its own class, so a `switch` can pick the right UI for it, such as a date picker for a day and a month picker for a month. `TimeRange` isn't sealed, so add a default case. Put the week cases before `CustomTimeRange`, since weeks are custom ranges too.")
B.YM=new A.H("`isAtSameYearAs` through `isAtSameMicrosecondAs` now convert `other` to the timezone of `this` and compare the start of the unit as an instant, like `isSame` in moment.js. In 5.x they compared the raw fields, so a UTC date and a local date of the same instant often didn't match. Now they always do:")
B.YN=new A.H("Like `DateTime`, moment_dart keeps the timezone it's given. Dates parsed from ISO 8601 strings or returned by an API are usually in UTC, so call `toLocal()` before you show them to a person. [Common mishaps](/common-mishaps) covers this and a few other traps.")
B.YP=new A.H("`MomentLocalization` is abstract. A subclass has to provide these members, though mixins can cover `relative`, `duration` and the calendar data for you.")
B.YQ=new A.H("Each setter clamps the value you pass, then calls `copyWith`. The other fields and the timezone stay as they are. The examples start from `DateTime(2023, 1, 31, 15, 9, 26)`.")
B.YR=new A.H("`MomentLocalization`, `FormatterToken`, `FormatSet`, `FormatSetOptional`, `CalendarLocalizationData`, `DurationInterval` and `Abbreviation` all come from the main library. The mixins, `UnitString` and `SimpleRangeData` are not exported, so they're imported from `src/`.")
B.YT=new A.H("The deprecated `Calender*` typedefs and `calender*` constants are gone. Use the `Calendar*` and `calendar*` names, for example `CalendarLocalizationData` and `LocalizationEnUs.calendarLocalizationDataEnUs`.")
B.YU=new A.H("`format` prints a moment in its own timezone, so a UTC moment prints UTC wall-clock time. Dates parsed from ISO 8601 strings or received from an API are often UTC. Set `forceLocal` to convert to local time before formatting. It's positional on `Moment.format` and the `format\u2026()` methods, and named on `DateTime.format`.")
B.YV=new A.H('The last two columns are computed live in your language. "Just before" is one second before the switch (one millisecond in the first row).')
B.YW=new A.H("Since 6.0, every end is computed as the start of the next unit minus one microsecond, not by setting the fields to 23:59:59.999999. Adding a microsecond to an end always gives the next start.")
B.YX=new A.H("Clocks in the United States and Canada moved forward on March 10, 2024. If that's your timezone, the difference is 6 days and 23 hours; elsewhere it's 7 days.")
B.YY=new A.H('`DateTime.copyWith` and the `DateTime` constructor accept out-of-range values and carry the overflow into the next unit. February 30 becomes March 1. That\'s useful for date math, but wrong when a user picks "day 30" for a monthly reminder and expects the last day of February.')
B.Z_=new A.H("Since 6.0, a single unit is written with its number in precise output, while relative output keeps the word:")
B.Z0=new A.H("`Moment(dateTime)` wraps a `DateTime` you already have. It keeps the same instant and the same timezone (UTC or local). `toMoment()` on a `DateTime` does the same thing.")
B.Z1=new A.H("If the date already falls on the weekday you ask for, the result is a week away: `nextThursday()` on a Thursday is the Thursday after, and `lastThursday()` is the one before. The result is never the same day.")
B.Z2=new A.H("To do the same from a date other than now, use the instance methods such as `date.startOfMonth()` on [startOf](/extension/startOf), [endOf](/extension/endOf) and the [relative finders](/extension/relativeFinders).")
B.Z4=new A.H("`Moment.now()` is the current time in the local timezone. `Moment.nowWithTimezone(true)` gives the current time in UTC.")
B.Z5=new A.H("Days are counted in the moment's own timezone; the reference is converted to it first. A UTC moment is compared by UTC days, which may not match the reader's day. Call `toLocal()` on dates from an API before you call `calendar()`.")
B.Z6=new A.H("`Moment` has static shortcuts that start from `DateTime.now()` and return a local `DateTime`: `startOfToday()`, `startOfTomorrow()`, `startOfYesterday()`, and `startOfThis\u2026`, `startOfNext\u2026`, `startOfLast\u2026` for `Hour`, `LocalWeek`, `IsoWeek`, `Month` and `Year`.")
B.Z7=new A.H("The last line shows the time in your timezone, so the two results match only if you're on UTC. `format` also takes `forceLocal: true` (positional on `Moment.format`, named on `DateTime.format`) for the same effect.")
B.Z8=new A.H('moment_dart doesn\'t bundle British English; `byLocale("en_GB")` falls back to American English. This localization reuses the American strings and changes the date order, the 24-hour clock and the week start.')
B.Z9=new A.H('`round` defaults to true and rounds the last unit to the nearest whole number. Set `round: false` to cut it off instead, which suits "time elapsed" displays that must never run ahead. The duration is rounded before it is split into units, so it carries over cleanly: 59 minutes 59.6 seconds is "1 hour", not "59 minutes 60 seconds".')
B.Za=new A.H("By default (`DurationFormat.auto`) the first row below that fits the duration is used, which gives its two largest units. You can also pass any of these formats yourself. A month counts as 30 days and a year as 365, regardless of the calendar.")
B.Zb=new A.H("In Flutter, look up the device locale and pass it to [byLocale](/localizations#by-locale). Since 6.0 it falls back to the closest localization of the same language, so `en-GB` or `pt-BR` still get English or Portuguese. Keep a fallback for languages moment_dart doesn't bundle.")
B.Zc=new A.H("A day isn't always 24 hours long. The lines below measure two days when clocks moved forward: March 10, 2024 in the United States and Canada, and March 31, 2024 in most of Europe. In those timezones the day is an hour shorter; elsewhere you'll see 24 hours less a microsecond.")
B.Zd=new A.H("Pass `isUtc: true` to get a UTC moment. Without it the result is in your local timezone, so the second line below depends on where you are.")
B.Ze=new A.H("The month range format includes the year. It's left out only when `useRelative` is true and the anchor is in the same year.")
B.Zf=new A.H('Order matters in the `with` clause: `SimpleUnits` must come before `SimpleRelative` and `SimpleDuration`, which are declared `on SimpleUnits`. `delimeterConnector` is spelled that way in the library; set it to a word like `"and"` to join the last two units.')
B.Zg=new A.H("`Moment.defaultLocalization` holds the current value. On this site the language picker in the header calls `setGlobalLocalization`, so the lines below follow it.")
B.Zh=new A.H("Check any boundary yourself:")
B.Zi=new A.H("`toCustom()`, or the `asCustom` getter, returns a `CustomTimeRange` with the same `from` and `to`. Use it when you want to adjust one end of a typed range. A range that is already custom is returned as is.")
B.Zk=new A.H("The results below depend on your timezone. The first line is the same everywhere; the rest show 12:00 UTC in your local time.")
B.Zm=new A.H("The options combine. For abbreviated units, add `form:` (see [short forms](/duration/short-forms)):")
B.Zn=new A.H("The local-week finders take the first day of the week as an optional `int`. Leave it out and a `DateTime` uses the global localization's week start (on this site, the language in the header), while a `Moment` uses its own localization. The ISO-week finders always use Monday.")
B.Zo=new A.H("Each localization describes itself, which is handy for a language picker. `endonym` is the language's name in that language, `languageNameInEnglish` is its English name, and `weekStart` is the first day of the week as a `DateTime` weekday constant. `weekStart` decides where local weeks begin in [startOfLocalWeek](/extension/startOf) and [LocalWeekTimeRange](/time-range).")
B.Zp=new A.H("`==` on `DateTime` is true only when both the instant and the timezone match. Use `isAtSameMomentAs` to compare instants.")
B.Zq=new A.H("The examples below run against that exact class.")
B.Zr=new A.H("Most helpers that return a date have a second version declared on `Moment`. Called on a `Moment`, they return a `Moment` with the same localization: `clone()`, `date`, the `startOf\u2026`/`endOf\u2026` methods, the next and last finders, the weekday finders and the clamped setters. Operators `+` and `-` return a `Moment` too.")
B.Zs=new A.H('`reference` defaults to now. Pass another date to describe a moment from a different day\'s point of view, for example the date a report was generated. The same moment that is "Today" from one reference is "Yesterday" from the next day.')
B.Zt=new A.H("The method converts `other` to the timezone of the date you call it on, takes the start of the unit for both, and compares those starts as instants. That matches `isSame` in moment.js.")
B.Zu=new A.H("To build that picker, go through `MomentLocalizations.locales`. Its keys are locale codes and each value returns the localization.")
B.Zv=new A.H("Chain `startOfDay()` when you need midnight of that day.")
B.Zw=new A.H("The extensions add operators that read better than method calls in conditions and arithmetic.")
B.Zx=new A.H("`intersect(other)`, or the `&` operator, returns the overlap as a `CustomTimeRange`, or null when the ranges don't overlap. If both ranges are the same span, you get the first one back unchanged.")
B.Zy=new A.H("In timezones two or more hours ahead of UTC, the second line is already 2025 locally, so its next year is 2026.")
B.Zz=new A.H("Weeks are only used by `DurationFormat.auto` when `includeWeeks` is true. The default differs: `fromPrecise` and `fromNowPrecise` include weeks, while `toDurationString` and `Moment.duration` don't.")
B.ZA=new A.H("`units` maps every `DurationInterval`, from `lessThanASecond` to `years`, to a `UnitString`. `UnitString.withForm(full, mid, short)` gives the three [abbreviation](/duration/short-forms) forms; `UnitString.duo` and `UnitString.single` cover languages with fewer. Write `srDelta` (`%` by default) where the number goes. `duration()` throws if an interval it needs is missing.")
B.ZC=new A.H("The [5.x docs](https://moment-dart-docs-v5.pages.dev) stay online while you upgrade.")
B.ZD=new A.H("A plain `DateTime` has `format` too. It takes named arguments, so you can also pick a localization for one call:")
B.ZE=new A.H("`Abbreviation` has three values. Every method on the [relative](/duration) and [precise](/duration/precise) pages takes one as `form:`, so short output works the same way everywhere.")
B.ZF=new A.H("Like the start helpers, `Moment` has static shortcuts that return a local `DateTime`: `endOfToday()`, `endOfTomorrow()`, `endOfYesterday()`, and `endOfThis\u2026`, `endOfNext\u2026`, `endOfLast\u2026` for `Hour`, `LocalWeek`, `IsoWeek`, `Month` and `Year`.")
B.ZG=new A.H("`fromPrecise(anchor)` and `fromNowPrecise()` are the exact counterparts of [`from` and `fromNow`](/duration). Use them when the amount matters, for example in a countdown or a log entry.")
B.ZH=new A.H("Pass it to a single moment as above, or make it the default for every moment with [global localization](/global-localization).")
B.ZI=new A.H('The last line converts to your timezone first, so its result depends on where you\'re reading this. Convert before you call a helper when you mean "the day as the user sees it".')
B.ZJ=new A.H('Pass `enforceUTC: true` to convert both dates to UTC before comparing. The answer then no longer depends on which date comes first or on the device\'s timezone. Use it for server-side grouping, or any time a "day" means a UTC day.')
B.ZK=new A.H("When you know the language at compile time, call its factory on `MomentLocalizations`. Each localization is created once, the first time you ask for it, and the same instance is returned after that.")
B.ZL=new A.H("`Moment.toString()` returns the human-readable `LLL` format in the moment's language, not ISO 8601. It loses the seconds and the timezone and can't be parsed back. Store and send `toIso8601String()` or `millisecondsSinceEpoch` instead.")
B.ZM=new A.H('For a `Duration` you already have, such as a video length or a timer, call `toDurationString()` or the static `Moment.duration()`. Both take a named `localization:` and fall back to the global one. A positive duration reads as future ("in \u2026") and a negative one as past, so pass `dropPrefixOrSuffix: true` for the bare amount.')
B.ZN=new A.H("The result is in the same timezone as the date you call it on. A UTC date gives UTC midnight; convert with `toLocal()` first if you want the start of the user's day.")
B.ZO=new A.H("Or add it to `pubspec.yaml` yourself:")
B.ZP=new A.H("If you don't use time ranges, nothing changes. In 5.0.1 the `to` of `HourTimeRange`, `DayTimeRange`, `MonthTimeRange` and `YearTimeRange` became inclusive: it's the last microsecond of the range instead of the start of the next one, matching `CustomTimeRange` and the week ranges. Their `duration` is one microsecond shorter as a result.")
B.ZQ=new A.H("On a `Moment`, every finder returns a `Moment` with the same localization.")
B.ZR=new A.H("Dates from APIs, ISO 8601 strings and generated clients are usually in UTC. `DateTime` keeps the timezone it was given, so formatting one shows UTC wall-clock time, which is rarely what the user expects. Convert with `toLocal()` first.")
B.ZS=new A.H("If the user can switch languages while the app runs, call `setGlobalLocalization` again and rebuild the widgets that show dates. moment_dart doesn't notify anything when the value changes.")
B.ZT=new A.H("The English name, endonym and week start below are read from each localization as the page renders. The class names follow the locale: `LocalizationDeDe`, `LocalizationMnMongMn` and so on.")
B.ZV=new A.H("On a `Moment`, each setter returns a `Moment` with the same localization. There is no clamped setter for milliseconds, microseconds or the year.")
B.ZW=new A.H("`copyWith` returns a new moment with some fields replaced. It takes the `DateTime` fields (`year` through `microsecond`, and `isUtc`), plus `dateTime` to replace the whole date and `localization` to replace the localization. When you pass both `dateTime` and fields, the fields are applied on top of `dateTime`.")
B.ZX=new A.H("`Moment` and `DateTime` are immutable. `add`, `subtract`, `copyWith` and the `startOf\u2026` methods return a new value; calling them without using the result does nothing.")
B.ZY=new A.H("Printing a moment calls `toString()`, which returns the `LLL` format in the moment's localization. Every constructor takes an optional `localization:`; without one, the moment uses the [global localization](/global-localization).")
B.ZZ=new A.H("Every name below also comes as `endOf\u2026`, for example `endOfLastMonth()`. The local week versions take an optional `weekStart` (`DateTime.monday` through `DateTime.sunday`) and default to the global localization's week start. ISO weeks always start on Monday.")
B.a__=new A.H("`Moment.copyWith` accepts `year` through `microsecond` and `isUtc`, applied on top of `dateTime` when you pass both. Before, it only took `dateTime` and `localization` and hid `DateTime.copyWith`. See [Creating moments](/moment#copywith).")
B.a_0=new A.H("The results use `dropPrefixOrSuffix: true` and your selected language.")
B.a_2=new A.H("A `Moment` prints itself with the localized `LLL` format, so the last line follows the language picked in the header.")
B.a_3=new A.H("Both tables start from `DateTime(2024, 3, 14, 15, 9, 26)`, a Thursday. Ends are the next start minus one microsecond, as on [end of a unit](/extension/endOf).")
B.a_4=new A.H("German, for example, has words for two days back and ahead:")
B.a_5=new A.H("Since 6.0 both forms keep UTC and `weekStart`, so a parsed range equals the one you encoded. Strings written by 5.x still parse the same way.")
B.a_6=new A.H("The `startOfLocalWeek` example passes `DateTime.sunday`. `startOf(DurationUnit.microsecond)` returns a copy of the date, since a microsecond is the smallest unit `DateTime` has.")
B.a_7=new A.H("`Moment.epoch`, `epochUtc`, `maxValue`, `maxValueUtc`, `minValue` and `minValueUtc` can no longer be reassigned.")
B.a_8=new A.H('`durationUnits` is new in 6.0. It defaults to `units`. Override it when the singular forms that read well in fuzzy time ("in a minute") read badly in precise durations ("2 hours a minute"). `en_US`, `de_DE`, `es_ES`, `fr_FR`, `it_IT`, `pt_PT` and `tr_TR` do this.')
B.a_9=new A.H("The results in these code blocks are computed in your browser as you read. Switch the language in the header and they follow.")
B.a_a=new A.H("Since 6.0, when there's no exact match it falls back to the closest localization of the same language, so `\"en_GB\"` gives English (United States). It returns `null` only when no bundled localization speaks that language. Pass `strict: true` to get `null` for anything that isn't an exact match.")
B.a_b=new A.H('Call `calendar()` on a `Moment`. Use it for timestamps people scan, like a chat list or an activity feed, where the day matters more than the exact gap. For "3 hours ago" style text, see [relative durations](/duration).')
B.a_c=new A.H("The helpers on these pages are Dart [extension methods](https://dart.dev/language/extension-methods) on `DateTime`. Importing `package:moment_dart/moment_dart.dart` brings them into scope, so you call them on any `DateTime` without wrapping it first.")
B.a_d=new A.H("Compare `add(Duration(days: 30))`, which counts 30 days of 24 hours and lands on March 1 here.")
B.a_e=new A.H("`MomentLocalizations.byLocale` now returns the closest localization of the same language when there's no exact match. In 5.x both lines below returned `null`. See [Localizations](/localizations#by-locale).")
B.a_f=new A.H("`DateTime` rolls extra days into the next month. Setting the month of January 31 to February gives a day in March. The [clamped setters](/extension/set-clamped) stop at the last day of the month instead.")
B.a_g=new A.H("Set `dropPrefixOrSuffix: true` to get only the amount. Use it when your own text supplies the direction, or when you need the size of a gap with no direction at all.")
B.a_h=new A.H("`Moment.startOfTomorrow()`, `Moment.endOfYesterday()`, `Moment.startOfNextMonth()` and the other static shortcuts call these finders on `DateTime.now()` and return a local `DateTime`.")
B.a_i=new A.H("`Moment` extends `DateTime`, so every extension works on a `Moment` as well.")
B.a_j=new A.H("When all you have is a `TimeRange`, check for `PageableRange`:")
B.a_k=new A.H("Precise output from `toDurationString(form: \u2026, dropPrefixOrSuffix: true)`:")
B.a_l=new A.H('`Duration` gets whole-unit getters to go with Dart\'s `inDays`. They use fixed lengths, not the calendar: a month is 30 days and a year is 365. For text like "2 hours 5 minutes", call `toDurationString()`, which takes the same options as [precise durations](/duration/precise).')
B.a_n=new A.H("Characters that aren't part of any token pass through unchanged. Digits, punctuation and most non-Latin scripts need no brackets, which makes patterns for languages like Korean or Japanese easy to read:")
B.a_o=new A.H("`differenceInDays` compares the calendar dates of two values as they are, without converting either one. That keeps it correct across daylight saving changes, but a UTC date and a local date of the same instant can be on different calendar days.")
B.a_p=new A.H("The two traditional Mongolian script localizations differ only in digits: `mn_Mong_MN` uses Western digits and `mn_Qaaq_MN` uses traditional Mongolian digits.")
B.a_r=new A.H("Set a duration and the options for [`toDurationString`](/duration/precise) and [`fromNow`](/duration). The code below the result updates with your choices.")
B.a_s=new A.H("`nextWeekday(int weekday)` and `lastWeekday(int weekday)` take the weekday as a number, for when it comes from settings or user input. Pass `DateTime.monday` (1) to `DateTime.sunday` (7).")
B.a_t=new A.H("See [Localizations](/localizations) for how lookups match, and [global localization](/global-localization) for making it the default.")
B.a_u=new A.H('`Duration(days: 1)` is 24 hours. On the day clocks change, adding it moves the time of day by an hour. When you mean "the same time tomorrow", change the calendar field instead.')
B.a_v=new A.H("Because `other` is converted to the receiver's timezone, swapping the two dates can change the answer when they are in different timezones. These two instants are an hour apart, on either side of midnight UTC.")
B.a_w=new A.H("`isPast` and `isFuture` compare the date with `DateTime.now()`. The anchored versions take the date to compare with as an optional positional argument, and fall back to now when you leave it out. A date equal to its anchor is neither past nor future.")
B.a_x=new A.H("Dart treats `src/` as private to a package, so the analyzer's `implementation_imports` lint flags these lines. Add `// ignore_for_file: implementation_imports` to the file, as the example does.")
B.a_y=new A.H("Removing the `Calender*` names and making the constants `final` are the only changes the compiler will catch. Comparisons across timezones, `byLocale`, `CustomTimeRange` and many localized strings now behave differently at runtime.")
B.a_z=new A.H("Type a format string or tap tokens to insert them. The full list, with descriptions, is in [formatter tokens](/formatting#formatter-tokens).")
B.a_A=new A.H("`timeZoneFormatted()` returns the offset as `+HH:mm`. Pass `false` to drop the colon. The first line shows your own offset.")
B.a_B=new A.H('`from(anchor)` measures from any date instead of now. The moment is "in \u2026" when it\'s after the anchor and "\u2026 ago" when it\'s before.')
B.a_C=new A.H("These lines call `toIso8601String()` because a `Moment` prints with the localized `LLL` format, which drops the seconds.")
B.a_D=new A.H("`DateTime.rangeTo(other)` makes a `CustomTimeRange` between two dates and swaps them if `other` comes first. `rangeToMax()` makes a range that never ends.")
B.a_E=new A.H("Calls that don't go through a moment with its own localization use the global one. That includes `Moment.duration`, `Duration.toDurationString` and `TimeRange.format` without `localization:`, and the week start of `DateTime.startOfLocalWeek()`, `endOfLocalWeek()` and `LocalWeekTimeRange` when you don't pass `weekStart`.")
B.a_F=new A.H("`toUtc()` on a typed range keeps its fields and switches the timezone. `YearTimeRange(2024).toUtc()` is the UTC year 2024, not the local year 2024 expressed in UTC, so its `from` differs from `from.toUtc()` unless you're in UTC. `CustomTimeRange.toUtc()` converts both instants instead. The second and third lines below depend on your timezone.")
B.a_G=new A.H("`Moment.epoch`, `maxValue` and `minValue` are local `DateTime`s, and each has a UTC twin (`epochUtc`, `maxValueUtc`, `minValueUtc`) for the same instant. `maxValue` and `minValue` are the largest and smallest dates Dart can represent, and are useful as open ends of a [time range](/time-range).")
B.a_H=new A.H("Format strings are made of [tokens](/formatting#formatter-tokens), and many single letters are tokens: `a` is am/pm, `D` is the day, `h` is the hour. Wrap literal text in square brackets.")
B.a_I=new A.H("`FormatSet` and `FormatSetOptional` come from the main library, so `overrideFormatters()` in a custom localization no longer needs an import from `src/`. The helper mixins still live under `src/`; see [Writing a localization](/creating-localization).")
B.a_J=new A.H('`MomentLocalizations.byLocale` takes a locale code such as a user setting or a device locale. Dashes and underscores both work, and matching ignores case. A bare language code like `"es"` first tries `es_ES`.')
B.a_K=new A.H('`L` and `l` have no getter; use `format("L")`. A moment\'s `toString()` returns `LLL`. Compare a few languages:')
B.a_L=new A.H("Results keep the timezone of the date you call them on; on a `Moment` they're `Moment`s with the same localization. Day, week, month and year finders work on calendar fields, so `startOfNextDay()` is midnight even when the day in between is 23 or 25 hours long. Hour and minute finders step through real instants, so across a fall-back transition `startOfNextHour()` is the next hour that actually happens, even when the clock shows the same hour again.")
B.a_M=new A.H("All examples in this table read `DateTime(2024, 3, 14, 15, 9, 26)`, a Thursday afternoon in a leap year. Getters read the date's own fields, so a UTC date answers for its UTC wall clock.")
B.a_N=new A.H('`fromNow()` compares a moment with the current time. Past moments get "ago", future ones get "in", each in the moment\'s language.')
B.a_O=new A.H("A `DateTime` has no localization to read a week start from, so `startOf(DurationUnit.week)` throws a `MomentException` on it. On a `Moment` it calls `startOfLocalWeek()` with the moment's localization.")
B.a_P=new A.H('Units are joined with the localization\'s separator, a space in most languages. Pass `delimiter` to use your own. Some languages join the last two units with a word such as "\u0438" in Russian.')
B.a_Q=new A.H('String interpolation calls `toString()` too, so `"$m"` is also the readable form.')
B.a_R=new A.H("`Moment.parse` and `Moment.tryParse` accept exactly what `DateTime.parse` accepts, mostly ISO 8601. There is no parsing by format string. A string ending in `Z` or an offset gives a UTC moment; without one, the string is read as local time.")
B.a_S=new A.H("The first line is what 5.x effectively did. It's `false` unless your timezone was at UTC+00:00 on that date. The other lines are `true` everywhere.")
B.a_T=new A.H('Relative text never says "`n` seconds" or uses weeks. Precise durations do both.')
B.zy=new A.H("The table starts from `DateTime(2024, 3, 14, 15, 9, 26, 535, 897)`, a Thursday.")
B.a_U=new A.H("`endOfLocalWeek` takes the first day of the week, not the last, so the same `weekStart` gives a matching start and end. It defaults to the global localization on a `DateTime` (on this site, the language in the header) and to the moment's localization on a `Moment`. `endOfIsoWeek()` always ends on Sunday.")
B.a_V=new A.H("`CustomTimeRange(from, to)` throws `ArgumentError` when `to` is before `from`. In 5.x this was only an assert, so release builds accepted reversed ranges. Use `rangeTo` when you don't know the order. `from` and `to` may be equal. Both should be in the same timezone; that is only checked by an assert, and `isUtc` follows `from`.")
B.a_X=new A.H("Week ranges can't convert: `toUtc()` throws `UnsupportedError`. Build them from a UTC date instead.")
B.a_Y=new A.H("Each cell is computed live in the language you picked in the header. Relative output, for a moment that long ago:")
B.a_Z=new A.H("Object patterns can read fields too, for example `MonthTimeRange(:final year, :final month)`.")
B.a0_=new A.H("From `DateTime(2024, 3, 14, 15, 9)`, a Thursday:")
B.a00=new A.H("For the end of a unit, see [end of a unit](/extension/endOf). For the unit after or before this one, see [next and last units](/extension/relativeFinders).")
B.a01=new A.H('`calendar()` counts calendar days between the moment and the reference, ignoring the time of day. 23:59 yesterday and 00:01 yesterday are both "Yesterday". Then:')
B.a02=new A.H("Every token `format` understands. When two tokens start at the same place the longer one wins, so `MMMM` is the month name rather than four month numbers. Values are English examples; the last column formats the current moment in the language you picked in the header.")
B.a04=new A.H('"Next" and "last" mean the neighboring calendar unit, not a fixed distance. `startOfNextMonth()` on January 31 is February 1, and `endOfNextMonth()` is the end of February, however many days it has.')
B.a05=new A.H("`MomentLocalization.formatters` is built once and cached, and now returns an unmodifiable map.")
B.a06=new A.H("Where a week starts depends on the locale: Sunday in the United States, Monday in most of Europe, Saturday in some Arabic-speaking countries. `startOfLocalWeek` takes the first day as an optional `int` (`DateTime.monday` to `DateTime.sunday`).")
B.a07=new A.H("`MomentLocalizations.byLanguage` takes a language code and optional `countryCode` and `scriptCode`, and returns the first localization that matches. If nothing matches, it drops the country code, then the script code, and tries again. `strict: true` turns that off.")
B.a08=new A.H("`duration` is `to.difference(from)`. Because `to` is the last microsecond of the range, it's one microsecond short of the length you might expect. Local ranges that cross a DST change are also an hour shorter or longer, which is why this example uses UTC.")
B.a09=new A.H("Each method takes the other date and an optional `enforceUTC` flag, and returns a `bool`. The examples compare `DateTime(2024, 3, 14, 15, 9, 26)` with `DateTime(2024, 3, 14, 15, 41)`, 31 minutes and 34 seconds later.")
B.a0a=new A.H("The local-week rows pass `DateTime.sunday`. There are no second or millisecond finders; use `startOfSecond() + Duration(seconds: 1)`.")
B.a0b=new A.H("`CustomTimeRange` throws an `ArgumentError` when `to` is before `from`. Before, this was only an assert, so release builds accepted the reversed range. `rangeTo` puts the two dates in order for you.")
B.a0c=new A.H("The `isAtSame\u2026As` methods work the other way since 6.0: they convert the other date into this one's timezone first and compare instants. See [isAtSame](/extension/isAtSame).")
B.a0d=new A.H("To count weeks from a localized week start (Sunday in the United States, for example), use [`startOfLocalWeek()`](/extension/startOf) instead.")
B.a0e=new A.H("A moment never changes. `add` and `subtract` return a new `Moment` and keep its localization. The `+` and `-` operators do the same, and also work on a plain `DateTime`.")
B.a0f=new A.H("Every range has `from`, `to`, `isUtc` and `unit`. The typed ranges store their calendar fields (year, month, \u2026) and build `from` and `to` from them.")
B.a0g=new A.H("The first line asks in UTC, where the two fall on different days. The second asks in your timezone, where both are usually on the same day; it's only `false` if your offset is within half an hour of UTC.")
B.a0h=new A.H("For text that should follow the reader's language, prefer the localized formats like `LLL` or `calendar()` over building your own sentence.")
B.a0i=new A.H("Pick a range type and step through it with `last` and `next`. See [time ranges](/time-range) for everything a range can do.")
B.a0j=new A.H("Past the six-day window, `calendar()` prints the date with the localization's `l` format. Pass `customFormat` to use another [format string](/formatting) there. With `customFormat` set, the result never includes the time, even for today or yesterday.")
B.a0k=new A.H("Many localized strings changed in 6.0. If your tests compare output text, see [Upgrading to 6.0](/upgrading). To add a language, see [Writing a localization](/creating-localization).")
B.a0l=new A.H("The explicit localization carries over to moments derived from it: `add`, `subtract`, `copyWith`, `toUtc`, `toLocal`, `startOf\u2026` and `endOf\u2026` all keep it. Wrapping in `Moment(...)` again drops it, which is how you hand a moment back to the global localization.")
B.a0n=new A.H("`setClampedMonth` is the only setter that touches a second field. Moving January 31 to February has to change the day too, so it clamps the day to the last day of the new month. Other days are kept.")
B.a0o=new A.H("Each mixin lives in `lib/src/localizations/mixins/` under the file name shown. Mixins that provide formatters do so through a getter; spread it into `overrideFormatters()`.")
B.a0q=new A.H("`MomentLocalizations.locales` is the map that `byLocale` and `byLanguage` search. Add your localization to it at startup. Formatters are cached per instance, so register one instance rather than a new one per call.")
B.a0r=new A.H("Two ranges are `==` when they have the same type, `from`, `to` and timezone:")
B.a0s=new A.H('In 5.x the first line read "in 2 hours a minute".')
B.a0t=new A.H("Set `omitHours: true` to drop the time, for example in a list grouped by day. Dates seven or more days away never show the time.")
B.a0u=new A.H("Each `startOf\u2026` method on [start of a unit](/extension/startOf) has an `endOf\u2026` counterpart. Use it for the inclusive upper bound of a range: everything up to and including the last microsecond of the day.")
B.a0w=new A.H("Every typed range mixes in `PageableRange`, whose `next` and `last` return the following and preceding range of the same type. They keep the timezone, and week ranges keep their `weekStart`. `CustomTimeRange` has no fixed length, so it can't page.")
B.a0x=new A.H("`contains(date)` is true when the date falls between `from` and `to`, both ends included. `containsRange(other)` is true when the other range fits entirely inside. Both compare instants, so mixing UTC and local dates is fine.")
B.a0y=new A.H("There is no `isAtSameWeekAs`, because a week's start depends on the locale. Use `isSameLocalWeekAs(other, [weekStart])`, which checks whether `other` falls between `startOfLocalWeek(weekStart)` and `endOfLocalWeek(weekStart)` of the receiver. Like the methods above, it compares instants, so `other` may be in either timezone.")
B.a0z=new A.H("The quickest route is to copy the bundled localization closest to your language from [lib/src/localizations](https://github.com/sadespresso/moment_dart/tree/master/lib/src/localizations) and change its strings. `en_US.dart` is the simplest. If your language is missing, consider sending it upstream so everyone gets it.")
B.a0A=new A.H("Dart counts 1 BC as year 0 and 44 BC as -43. `YYYY` prints that number, while `y` prints the year within its era. `X` rounds toward the past, so half a second before the epoch is -1:")
B.a0B=new A.H("Since 6.0 it compares the year, month and day fields directly. Before, it was off by one across a 23-hour day at the start of daylight saving time.")
B.a0C=new A.H("`TimeRange.fromStartAndDuration(start, duration)` makes a `CustomTimeRange` from `start` to `start + duration`.")
B.a0D=new A.H('`omitZeros` defaults to true, which leaves out units that come to zero. Set it to false for a fixed layout. When every unit is zero the result is the localization\'s "a few seconds".')
B.a0E=new A.H("`encode()` (also what `toString()` returns) writes the type and ISO 8601 dates. `encodeShort()` writes a compact form: a type letter, `from` as base-36 microseconds since the epoch, and a flags part when needed (`u` for UTC, a digit 1 to 7 for a week's `weekStart`). `TimeRange.parse` reads both and returns the original type; `TimeRange.tryParse` returns null instead of throwing.")
B.a0F=new A.H("The second line is the 5.x-style comparison of calendar dates as they are. Its result depends on your timezone: `false` east of UTC, `true` elsewhere. More on the methods in [isAtSame](/extension/isAtSame).")
B.a0G=new A.H("The getters (`LL`, `LT`, ...) have no `forceLocal` flag. Call `toLocal()` first, as in the fourth line.")
B.a0H=new A.H("Hour, minute, second and millisecond ends step to the next start of that unit after the date, so they also work where an offset changes by half an hour.")
B.a0I=new A.H("Like `DateTime.copyWith`, `isUtc: true` keeps the wall-clock fields and reinterprets them as UTC; it doesn't convert. Use `toUtc()` to convert. Out-of-range fields roll over, so `copyWith(month: 2)` on January 31 lands in March. The [clamped setters](/extension/set-clamped) stop at the end of the month instead.")
B.a0J=new A.H("`format()` describes a range in words, relative to `anchor` (now by default). Set `useRelative: false` for absolute dates, and pass `localization:` to override the global language.")
B.a0K=new A.H("The second line follows the language in the header. The extension is chosen by static type: a `Moment` held in a `DateTime` variable throws too.")
B.a0L=new A.H('`differenceInDays(other)` counts calendar days between two dates and ignores the time of day. It\'s positive when `other` is earlier. Use it for "3 days ago" style labels, where two minutes across midnight should count as a day and 23 hours on the same day should not.')
B.a0M=new A.H("The default is English (United States). Call `Moment.setGlobalLocalization` when your app starts, before anything is formatted.")
B.a0N=new A.H("To include today, check `weekday` first: `d.weekday == DateTime.thursday ? d : d.nextThursday()`.")
B.a0O=new A.H('Many localizations were corrected: Russian and Polish grammar, Arabic plural forms, English long dates ("January 5, 2024"), German 24-hour times, the Unicode 16 encoding for traditional Mongolian script, and more. The full list is in the [changelog](https://github.com/sadespresso/moment_dart/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md).')
B.a0P=new A.H("The `TimeRange` static constructors build common ranges from the current time, in the local timezone. The week constructors take an optional `weekStart`.")
B.a0Q=new A.H('Build a `DurationFormat` from a list of `DurationUnit`s, largest first, to show exactly those units. This is how you get "13 days left" instead of "1 week 6 days", or hours past 24. The units are `year`, `month`, `week`, `day`, `hour`, `minute` and `second`; anything smaller than a second isn\'t supported.')
B.a0R=new A.H("You can also override `weekStart` (defaults to Monday), `locale` (defaults to `languageCode_countryCode`; override it to add a script code, as `mn_Mong_MN` does), `calendarTime` (the time part of `calendar()`), `calendar()` and `range()`.")
B.a0S=new A.H("The comparisons cascade: if two dates are in the same day, they are also in the same month and year, and the same hour implies the same day. Each method checks the whole unit, not one field: `isAtSameDayAs` is false for March 14 and April 14.")
B.a0T=new A.H("`weekday` is 1 for Monday and 7 for Sunday. German weeks start on Monday, American weeks on Sunday.")
B.a0U=new A.H("The `endOfLocalWeek` example passes `DateTime.sunday`, so the week ends on Saturday. `endOf(DurationUnit.microsecond)` returns a copy of the date.")
B.a0V=new A.H("On a `Moment`, `date` returns a `Moment` with the same localization.")
B.a0W=new A.H('Use these to schedule "next Tuesday" or to find the most recent payday. The result keeps the hour, minute and second of the date you start from, and its timezone.')
B.a0X=new A.H("A bracketed part ends at the first `]`, so brackets don't nest, and empty brackets `[]` print as they are.")
B.a0Z=new A.H("The first result is `-1` east of UTC and `0` elsewhere. Put both dates in the same timezone before counting days, usually the local one.")
B.a1_=new A.H("All examples below use Wednesday, March 13, 2024 at 9:00 as the reference, so they don't change from day to day.")
B.a10=new A.H("A month past 12 becomes December of the same year, not January of the next. Use `startOfNextMonth()` and the other [relative finders](/extension/relativeFinders) to move between months.")
B.a11=new A.H("`Moment` has static methods for the start and end of common periods around now. They return a plain `DateTime` in the local timezone, not a `Moment`, so call `toMoment()` when you want to format the result.")
